   52270/   25F0A :                     
   52271/   25F0A :                     ; ==========================================================
   52272/   25F0A :                     ; Main Game Script Area
   52273/   25F0A :                     ;
   52274/   25F0A :                     ; The text block to load and display is determined by the value in the "script_offset" RAM location.
   52275/   25F0A :                     ; The offset is simply the GameScript location + the value present in the "script_offset".
   52276/   25F0A :                     ;
   52277/   25F0A :                     ; Here's some explanation about what data represents here:
   52278/   25F0A :                     ;
   52279/   25F0A :                     ; Bytes 1-2 = This is another offset value relative to the GameScript address and it's used when a particular event is set and the offset for
   52280/   25F0A :                     ;			  the event is determined by the 3rd byte below this script offset
   52281/   25F0A :                     ; Byte 3 = This is the offset relative to the "event_flags" RAM location and, if the byte at that location is set, then the offset specified in the first
   52282/   25F0A :                     ;		   2 bytes is loaded and that one will be used and the process starts all over. If you want to use the current text block, then set it to $FF
   52283/   25F0A :                     ; Byte 4 = This byte will set an event flag at the offset determined by this byte relative to the "event_flags" RAM location. If no event is triggered by
   52284/   25F0A :                     ;		   this text, then it's set to $00
   52285/   25F0A :                     ;
   52286/   25F0A :                     ; The following data is the actual text. Here's some characters that manipulate the text:
   52287/   25F0A :                     ;
   52288/   25F0A :                     ; $F8 = Moves the following text data to a new line. Only used once per text block
   52289/   25F0A :                     ; $EC = Clears the current text and displays the following text data
   52290/   25F0A :                     ; $FC = End of text
   52291/   25F0A :                     ;
   52292/   25F0A :                     ; Creating new dialogues for existing characters is very easy. You simply need to know the flag for a particular event and then decide if to move to a new
   52293/   25F0A :                     ; text offset. So let's take an existing block as an example:
   52294/   25F0A :                     ;
   52295/   25F0A :                     ;loc_27894:
   52296/   25F0A :                     ;	dc.w	loc_278F2-GameScript
   52297/   25F0A :                     ;	dc.b	EventFlag_MieuJoined, EventFlag_MieuTrigger
   52298/   25F0A :                     ;	dc.b	"I saw one of Laya's"
   52299/   25F0A :                     ;	dc.b	$F8
   52300/   25F0A :                     ;	dc.b	"people at a lake by the"
   52301/   25F0A :                     ;	dc.b	$EC
   52302/   25F0A :                     ;	dc.b	"northeastern forest. The"
   52303/   25F0A :                     ;	dc.b	$EC
   52304/   25F0A :                     ;	dc.b	"woman never blinked!"
   52305/   25F0A :                     ;	dc.b	$FC
   52306/   25F0A :                     ;
   52307/   25F0A :                     ; So after reaching this text section, the code stores the text offset in case a flag event is set. In this case the byte is $09, which means it checks for the
   52308/   25F0A :                     ; event flag at location "event_flags+9"; if the event is set ($FF), then it loads the new offset and goes to loc_278F2 in this case.
   52309/   25F0A :                     ; Then the next byte which is another event flag offset will be used to set another event, in this case it will set the byte at location "event_flags+8".
   52310/   25F0A :                     ; This text block makes Mieu appear on the world map, basically. Then when you get Mieu, the flag at location "event_flags+9" will be set, thus this NPC will say
   52311/   25F0A :                     ; a different dialogue... The one loaded in the first 2 bytes.
   52312/   25F0A :                     ;
   52313/   25F0A :                     ; You can scroll through this section and get a general idea of the different event flags; it should be relatively easy to guess what some event flags do if you
   52314/   25F0A :                     ; know the game fairly well. In any case you can always play using a RAM viewer and track the values in RAM location "$FFFFBF00" through "$FFFFBFFF".
   52315/   25F0A :                     ; ==========================================================
   52316/   25F0A :                     GameScript:
   52317/   25F0A :                     
   52318/   25F0A :                     loc_25F0A:
   52319/   25F0A : 0000                	dc.w	0
   52320/   25F0C : FF00                	dc.b	$FF, $00
   52321/   25F0E : 4E6F 7468 696E 6720 	dc.b	"Nothing unusual here."
                    756E 7573 7561 6C20 
                    6865 7265 2E        
   52322/   25F23 : FC                  	dc.b	$FC
   52323/   25F24 :                     
   52324/   25F24 : (MACRO)             	even
   52325/   25F24 :                     
   52326/   25F24 :                     loc_25F24:
   52327/   25F24 : 0000                	dc.w	0
